WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:03.909
Stan Lee is far from the only person to make
a Marvel cameo.

00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:08.300
In fact, many of the most interesting MCU
cameos come from people whom casual moviegoers

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.900
may never have heard of or expected to see
in a superhero movie.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:16.920
Avengers: Endgame is a massive film packed
with details everywhere you look, but some

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:21.070
of its most exciting tidbits and Easter eggs
arrive not with the big action spectacles,

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.960
but with the quiet moments.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.660
Early in the film, Steve Rogers AKA Captain
America is in New York City, attending a support

00:00:28.669 --> 00:00:32.680
group for people still struggling to find
a way to move on after Thanos wiped out half

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:33.300
of all life.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:38.790
The scene mostly revolves around a man, played
by Endgame co-director Joe Russo, explaining

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:42.280
a recent date he had, but if you look at the
other people sitting in that circle, you'll

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.320
notice a bald man with a goatee.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:49.420
That's Jim Starlin, an extremely influential
comic book writer and artist who's best known

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:55.090
as the creator of Thanos as well as Drax and
Gamora, among many others and the author of

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:59.870
the most important Thanos story, The Infinity
Gauntlet, the tale in which Thanos first snaps

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.180
his fingers to wipe out half of everyone.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.800
So Thanos' snap did not erase the man who
dreamed him into existence.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.620
Every Marvel Cinematic Universe film is packed
with Easter eggs, callbacks, cameos, and other

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:17.450
details you can't possibly absorb on the first
watch, but the Guardians of the Galaxy films

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:21.380
are particularly packed, even when compared
with other MCU releases.

00:01:21.869 --> 00:01:26.480
Writer/director James Gunn is particularly
committed to his Easter eggs and cameos, and,

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.369
as a result, the Guardians flicks are full
of them.

00:01:29.369 --> 00:01:33.110
Some, like Nathan Fillion's appearance as
a prisoner in the first Guardians film, are

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:34.320
fairly well known.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:39.320
Others are less so, in part because they often
take the form of seemingly random voices.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:44.209
In a post-credits scene from Guardians of
the Galaxy Vol. 2, the Ravager leader Stakar

00:01:44.209 --> 00:01:48.619
is looking to reform his old team, which is
made up of members of the original Guardians

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:52.880
of the Galaxy, whose Marvel Comics origins
date back to 1969.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.740
Among them is a robotic head named Mainframe,
who is voiced by none other than Miley Cyrus.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:01.220
"I missed you guys so much!"

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:06.700
Miley Cyrus isn't the only music star to cameo
in the Guardians of the Galaxy universe.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:11.120
In the first film, we hear a voice on the
Ravagers' ship dubbed the "Ravager Navigator."

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.260
"Ronan's fleet has been spotted and will arrive
in t-minus 15 minutes."

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.520
That voice could have been anyone, including
some random member of the crew, but since

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:24.700
this is a James Gunn movie, he managed to
use the voice of one of his friends.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:30.440
This time around, it was rock star and director
of House of 1000 Corpses and The Devil's Rejects,

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:31.060
Rob Zombie.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:32.440
Gunn explained:

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.340
"Rob's a friend, and he appeared in my first
movie, Slither, so now it's become my signature.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.940
But I love putting a lot of stuff in my movies
for fans.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:43.939
In Slither, every street sign referenced a
classic horror film.

00:02:43.939 --> 00:02:45.200
There were hundreds of them.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.740
I'm in service to those people who want to
watch a movie again and again."

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:54.160
Zombie also made a cameo as an "Unseen Ravager"
in Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:01.020
In X-Men: Days of Future Past, Sentinel inventor
Bolivar Trask appears before a congressional

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:04.959
committee to discuss his solutions to the
perceived issues stemming from the mutant

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:05.620
population.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:10.620
The congressmen are divided in their opinions,
with some believing mutants are largely peaceful

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.239
and others claiming they're a legitimate threat.

00:03:13.239 --> 00:03:17.840
One of the congressmen arguing for peace is
Chris Claremont, the undisputed most important

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.250
X-Men writer ever.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:23.540
"We have very real enemies out there the Russians,
the Chinese."

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:28.890
Claremont took over Uncanny X-Men in 1975
and remained the series' writer throughout

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:34.189
the rest of the 1970s and '80s, while also
co-creating various spinoff series and standalone

00:03:34.189 --> 00:03:35.820
graphic novels along the way.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:41.080
Many of the greatest X-Men storylines ever,
including "Days of Future Past," "God Loves,

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:46.310
Man Kills," and "The Dark Phoenix Saga," were
his creation, and he co-created X-Men characters

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:48.920
ranging from Mystique to Gambit to Jubilee.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:54.150
He also made a cameo as a man mowing his lawn
in X-Men: The Last Stand, but in Days of Future

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.040
Past, he actually gets some dialogue.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.540
Claremont's not the only major figure from
superhero comics to appear in Days of Future

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:02.180
Past.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:06.720
He's not even the only major figure from superhero
comics to appear in that congressional hearing

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:07.220
scene.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.260
One of the other congressmen, who at one point
argues with Claremont's character, is none

00:04:11.269 --> 00:04:16.090
other than Len Wein, another pivotal figure
in the history of the X-Men and in superhero

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.150
comics as a whole.

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:19.709
"We haven't had an incident in ten years."

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:20.709
"After what happened in Cuba?"

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:22.069
"That was never confirmed."

00:04:22.069 --> 00:04:26.120
Wein is a key figure in the histories of both
Marvel and DC Comics.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:30.800
He was Marvel's editor in chief for a brief
period in the mid-'70s, and in 1975 he was

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:35.930
the writer tasked with reviving the X-Men,
a team that had been relegated to reprints

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:37.600
for several years at that point.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:42.140
Working with artist Dave Cockrum, Wein devised
a strategy to bring the team back with a new

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:44.020
group of international members.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:48.400
He soon handed writing duties over to Claremont,
but Wein's jolt of energy at this pivotal

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.480
time in X-Men history was critical.

00:04:50.669 --> 00:04:55.160
He was also a vital character influence on
the makeup of the eventual core team, having

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.740
co-created Wolverine, Storm, Nightcrawler,
Colossus, and more.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:04.500
For a very long time in the world of comic
books, James Buchanan “Bucky" Barnes was

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:09.220
a character you could count on to stay dead,
alongside former Robin Jason Todd and Peter

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:10.540
Parker's Uncle Ben.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:15.220
He was Captain America's World War II pal,
but Cap had to move on without him as he tried

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:16.760
to exist in a new era.

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:22.600
Then, in 2005, Bucky returned as the Winter
Soldier, a Soviet assassin with a metal arm.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.970
The storyline became one of the most popular
and acclaimed Marvel Comics runs of the 2000s,

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:31.560
and ultimately made the leap to the big screen
in the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:36.280
These days, Bucky Barnes is one of the MCU
fandom's favorite characters, so it's only

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:39.980
fitting that at least one of his creators
should get a little nod on the big screen.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.380
In Captain America: The Winter Soldier, during
one of the scenes in the Hydra lab, you'll

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:49.240
see writer Ed Brubaker, who co-created the
Winter Soldier with artist Steve Epting, helping

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:52.440
to work on Bucky as Alexander Pierce interrogates
him.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:58.400
Marvel Studios' first Thor film was teased
at the end of Iron Man 2 with a post-credits

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:02.750
sequence that featured Agent Phil Coulson
finding the God of Thunder's hammer embedded

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:04.900
in a crater in the New Mexico desert.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:09.380
That crater creates an interesting bit of
chaos in Thor, as the locals find the hammer

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:12.810
and start taking turns trying to pull it out
of the ground before S.H.I.E.L.D. manages

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:13.960
to quarantine it.

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:17.960
This, of course, culminates in the moment
when a man played by Stan Lee himself tries

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.980
to yank the thing loose with a truck.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:20.620
It doesn't work.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:26.220
"Did it work?"

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.820
Before Lee's truck antics, though, another
key Thor writer attempts to pry the hammer

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:35.319
out of the ground: J. Michael Straczynski,
author of an acclaimed and influential run

00:06:35.319 --> 00:06:39.750
on the main Thor title in the late 2000s,
making him one of the biggest names associated

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.140
with the character around the time the film
was made.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:47.460
Straczynski isn't the only major Thor writer
to make a cameo in the MCU.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:52.080
Later in the first Thor film, after the battle
on Midgard is won, Lady Sif and the Warriors

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.540
Three are celebrating their victory with a
feast in the halls of Asgard.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:00.330
As Volstagg tells an amusing story about their
exploits, the camera cuts to Lady Sif and

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:03.400
an unnamed Asgardian man as they laugh in
response.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:08.099
That man is Walt Simonson, and if anyone has
the right to be hanging out in Asgard with

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:09.640
the Warriors Three, it's him.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:15.010
Simonson is perhaps the most influential and
acclaimed Thor writer/artist of all time,

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:20.800
thanks to an epic run on Thor from 1983 to
1987, which introduced many of the series'

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:25.500
most popular supporting characters and storylines,
including Beta Ray Bill and that time Thor

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:27.000
got turned into a frog.

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:31.320
His fingerprints are all over the various
Thor films, and his impact is still felt in

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.400
Thor comics today.

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:37.820
Some Marvel movie cameos happen because the
person making the cameo is a recognizable

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:40.160
movie star who might get the crowd excited.

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:44.260
Others serve as tributes to comic book creators
without whom the characters and storylines

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:46.200
featured in the films would not exist.

00:07:46.629 --> 00:07:50.560
Then there are those blink-and-you'll-miss-them
cameos that make you look twice because you're

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:55.700
not sure if you saw the seemingly random celebrity
or just someone who kinda looks like them.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:08:00.490
That's the case with one particular cameo
in the MCU movie that started it all, Iron

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:00.980
Man.

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:05.680
As Tony Stark fires up his first ever Iron
Man armor to stage his escape from the cave

00:08:05.699 --> 00:08:10.400
where he's been kept prisoner, his arc reactor
shines onto the face of a seemingly random

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:13.020
terrorist, who is then quickly swatted away.

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:18.640
That early victim of Iron Man was Rage Against
the Machine and Audioslave guitarist Tom Morello,

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.280
who later confirmed his involvement on social
media.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:26.560
In Deadpool 2, Deadpool decides he needs to
put together a super team to pull off his

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:28.340
new mission of stopping Cable.

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:33.060
He assembles a group of mutants and a guy
named Peter for the job, including Domino,

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:35.900
Shatterstar, Bedlam, and someone called the
Vanisher.

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:40.340
The Vanisher is appropriately named, because
we almost never see or hear him.

00:08:40.349 --> 00:08:44.940
He's just an invisible presence in every scene,
until the moment when X-Force's parachute

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:49.780
jump goes horribly wrong and several of the
would-be heroes die gruesome deaths.

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:54.700
The Vanisher, still attached to his parachute,
flies into some power lines and is electrocuted.

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:59.339
At the moment the electricity touches him,
we see that the Vanisher is actually Brad

00:08:59.340 --> 00:08:59.840
Pitt.

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:04.720
It's a split-second cameo, but it's one of
the biggest surprises in all of superhero

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:05.220
cinema.

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:05.980
How did it happen?

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:10.800
Well, according to screenwriter Paul Wernick,
Ryan Reynolds himself reached out to Pitt,

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:15.510
whose kids are Deadpool fans, after they agreed
it would be funny to get an extremely difficult-to-book

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:16.580
actor for the cameo.

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:19.340
And the rest, friends, is cinematic history.

00:09:19.340 --> 00:09:20.340
“It’s in the movie.

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:24.360
It's just a quarter of a second, it's Brad
Pitt, he's the Vanisher, and he unfortunately

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:25.360
dies by electrocution.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:25.860
Yeah."

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:31.000
Pitt isn't the only A-lister to make a difficult-to-spot
cameo in Deadpool 2.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:35.200
At one point in the film, Cable approaches
a pair of locals as they're having a discussion

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:38.870
in the back of their truck about, of all things,
toilet paper.

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:42.640
The point of the scene narratively centers
around Cable stealing the truck, but it's

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.020
also a platform for some hilarious dialogue
about the inadequacy of toilet paper.

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:50.540
"Toilet paper is a plenty fine appetizer.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:51.500
But then…

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:55.680
Huggies Natural Care Wet Wipes.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:56.840
That's your main course."

00:09:57.269 --> 00:10:02.260
One of the men in the conversation is fairly
easy to recognize as Firefly star Alan Tudyk.

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:04.880
The other, under heavy makeup, is Matt Damon.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:09.440
Deadpool 2 director David Leitch later explained
how they pulled that off, saying:

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:12.980
"Bill Corso, our makeup artist, he's a master
at prosthetics.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:16.540
He had Matt in the chair for three hours,
and a lot of the crew didn't know.

00:10:16.870 --> 00:10:19.060
There were some people who had worked with
him on movies…

00:10:19.079 --> 00:10:23.399
But we didn't even tell the crew, his name
wasn't on the call sheet it was a fake name.

00:10:23.399 --> 00:10:26.490
Nobody really knew what that scene was about,
they were like, 'Why are we shooting these

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:27.600
two rednecks?'

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:28.880
We just didn't tell anybody."

